;;; cl-lambda-lists.lisp: lambda lists for the operators of the COMMON-LISP package
;;;
;;; DOTCL:FUNCTION-LAMBDA-LIST answers these for the external symbols of
;;; COMMON-LISP that name a function, generic function, macro or special
;;; operator, and for the function objects those symbols name. Most of the
;;; standard functions are written in C# and record no lambda list of their own;
;;; the ones written in Lisp record their implementation's parameter names.
;;; This table gives all of them the lambda list of their dictionary entry.
;;;
;;; Names follow the Syntax section of each entry. A parameter written there as
;;; THING+ (one or more) is THING &REST MORE-THINGS. Defaults and supplied-p
;;; variables are left out. A macro or special operator whose syntax is a
;;; grammar rather than a lambda list gets the macro lambda list conventionally
;;; used for it: a body of declarations and forms is &BODY, a list destructured
;;; by the operator is a nested list.
;;;
;;; This file is data only. It sets *CL-BUILTIN-LAMBDA-LISTS* (declared in
;;; cil-stdlib.lisp) to a function of no arguments returning the table, so
;;; loading the core only defines that function. Each entry is
;;; (SYMBOL-NAME "PARAMETERS"), the text inside the outer parentheses, in plain
;;; ASCII. test/regression/cl-builtin-lambda-lists.lisp checks that every fbound
;;; external CL symbol has an entry and that each entry agrees with the
;;; function where the function records a lambda list of its own.

(setq *cl-builtin-lambda-lists* (lambda ()
 '(("*" "&REST NUMBERS")
   ("+" "&REST NUMBERS")
   ("-" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
   ("/" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
   ("/=" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
   ("1+" "NUMBER")
   ("1-" "NUMBER")
   ("<" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
   ("<=" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
   ("=" "NUMBER &REST MORE-NUMBERS")
